## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical thinking on append-heavy data structures, cache-aware trade-offs, and I/O modeling under realistic constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Two-tier indexing
- Amortized cost analysis
- Cache & I/O modeling
- Rebuild strategies and hot/cold separation

## Code Example
```javascript
class TieredIndex {
  constructor(B) { this.B=B; this.t1=new Map(); this.t2=new Map(); }
  append(blockId, offset) { this.t1.set(blockId, offset); /* trigger rebuild when needed */ }
  lookup(blockId) { if (this.t1.has(blockId)) return this.t1.get(blockId); return this.t2.get(blockId); }
  rebuild() { for (let [k,v] of this.t1) this.t2.set(k,v); this.t1.clear(); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this adapt if blocks could be deleted or updated? 
- How would you empirically validate the cost model with real workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about fixed-memory distinct counting in streaming data, a common real-world need for analytics in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for cardinality estimation
- Mergeable sketches across batches
- Relative error ~ 1.04/√m; memory fixed by m

## Code Example
```javascript
function hllAdd(registers, x, hash) {
  const m = registers.length;
  const i = hash & (m - 1);
  const w = hash >>> Math.log2(m);
  const rank = leadingZeros(w) + 1;
  registers[i] = Math.max(registers[i], rank);
}
```

